{"method":"item/completed","params":{"item":{"type":"commandExecution","id":"call_Zd84Dc0YggZZvwIAInPdA7NX","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -c 'rg -n \"cargo (build|check|run).*(device|riscv|kernel-device)|target\" README.md docs -S'","cwd":"/Users/egv/dev/PaperDOS/.yolo-runner/clones/bd-976e.1","processId":"31582","status":"completed","commandActions":[{"type":"search","command":"rg -n 'cargo (build|check|run).*(device|riscv|kernel-device)|target' README.md docs -S","query":"cargo (build|check|run).*(device|riscv|kernel-device)|target","path":"README.md"}],"aggregatedOutput":"README.md:17:├── kernel/                     # PaperDOS kernel (Rust, targeting ESP32-C3)\nREADME.md:81:- **Rust** (stable >= 1.88) with `riscv32imc-unknown-none-elf` target\ndocs/PRD_Web_Flasher.md:68:Rust firmware compiled for ESP32-C3 via `cargo build --release --target riscv32imc-unknown-none-elf` produces a standard ELF binary. The `espflash save-image` command converts this to a flashable binary containing the bootloader, partition table, and app image. This merged .bin file is exactly what esptool-js expects.\ndocs/PRD_Web_Flasher.md:75:    target/riscv32imc-unknown-none-elf/release/paperdos \\\ndocs/PRD_Web_Flasher.md:85:| Chrome (desktop) | Yes (v89+) | Primary target |\ndocs/PRD_Web_Flasher.md:199:#[cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")]\ndocs/PRD_Web_Flasher.md:208:pub fn convert_image(image_bytes: &[u8], target_width: u32, target_height: u32, grayscale_levels: u8) -> Vec<u8> {\ndocs/PaperDOS_Technical_Design_v0.1.md:103:The ESP32-C3 has approximately 400 KB of SRAM. PulpOS reports ~172 KB usable heap after system initialization (108 KB main + 64 KB reclaimed after Wi-Fi teardown). PaperDOS targets a similar layout, with the strip-based rendering approach eliminating the need for a full framebuffer.\ndocs/PaperDOS_Technical_Design_v0.1.md:137:The kernel is written in **Rust (no_std)** targeting `riscv32imc-unknown-none-elf`. This choice is validated by two community firmware projects (PulpOS and TernReader) that demonstrate Rust is production-ready on the X4's ESP32-C3. Key advantages over C/C++:\ndocs/PaperDOS_Technical_Design_v0.1.md:450:Apps compile with `cargo build --target riscv32imc-unknown-none-elf` using the same linker script, then run through `pdpack.py` to produce a .pdb. The entry point must be `#[no_mangle] extern \"C\" fn pd_main(sys: *const Syscalls)`.\ndocs/PaperDOS_Technical_Design_v0.1.md:484:$ cargo build --release --target riscv32imc-unknown-none-elf\ndocs/PaperDOS_Technical_Design_v0.1.md:485:$ python3 pdpack.py target/riscv32imc-unknown-none-elf/release/hello hello.pdb \\\ndocs/PaperDOS_Technical_Design_v0.1.md:496:- **CI validation.** The `pdpack.py` tool includes a `--validate` flag that checks header integrity, relocation sanity, and estimated memory usage against the target device.\ndocs/PaperDOS_Technical_Design_v0.1.md:748:Several open-source firmware projects targeting the Xteink X4 have emerged. Two Rust-based projects are particularly relevant to PaperDOS and have directly informed its architecture: PulpOS and TernReader.\ndocs/PaperDOS_Technical_Design_v0.1.md:752:PulpOS is a minimal, bare-metal e-ink OS written entirely in Rust (no_std). It targets the same ESP32-C3 + SSD1677 hardware and is the most technically sophisticated community firmware.\n","exitCode":0,"durationMs":0},"threadId":"019d5442-09af-7ba0-9081-56c96a87ed6b","turnId":"019d5442-09b2-76d1-b68b-88d956769067"}}
